Yeah—this should work like an ensemble caper where everybody is funny for a different reason, and the mission is just serious enough that they all think their particular skill set is essential.

The mission began in a conference room at a Hampton Inn outside Pittsburgh.

Nobody knew why.

Michael Scott stood at the front beside a projector screen displaying:

OPERATION: FREEDOM BIRD

Below it was a clip-art eagle wearing sunglasses.

Dave Chappelle stared at the screen.

“Before we even start, I need everybody to understand that whoever let this man make the PowerPoint has already compromised the operation.”

Michael pointed at him.

“Incorrect. Visual communication is sixty percent of leadership.”

“According to who?”

“Me.”

“That’s what I was afraid of.”

Around the table sat Adam Sandler, Jim Carrey, Will Ferrell, Ace Ventura, Danny DeVito, and one extremely confused park ranger named Susan who had been told this was an interagency briefing.

It was not.

Michael clicked the remote.

A photograph appeared of a bald eagle named President Larry.

President Larry was the beloved mascot of the Allegheny Wildlife Education Center.

He had been stolen the previous evening.

Ace Ventura shot to his feet.

“STOLEN.”

“Yes,” said Susan.

“From a secured aviary?”

“Yes.”

“With cameras?”

“Yes.”

“Feathers?”

“Presumably.”

Ace slapped both hands on the table.

“This bird has been bird-napped.”

Dave looked at him.

“You had five seconds and chose that.”

Ace leaned toward him.

“It chose me.”

Adam Sandler was eating peanut M&Ms from the hotel minibar.

“So who steals an eagle?”

Will Ferrell suddenly stood.

“A rival nation.”

Everyone stared.

“No,” said Susan.

“You don’t know that.”

“We have surveillance footage.”

Ferrell slowly sat down.

“Then why are we still talking?”

Michael clicked again.

Security footage appeared.

At 2:13 a.m., a person wearing a giant groundhog costume rolled a laundry cart through the wildlife center.

Inside the cart was President Larry.

Dave leaned toward the screen.

“So a groundhog kidnapped an eagle.”

Susan sighed.

“A person dressed as a groundhog.”

Danny DeVito squinted.

“Could be a union thing.”

Nobody knew what he meant.

Nobody asked.

Michael clicked to the next slide.

THE SUSPECT

A photograph appeared of an enormous abandoned amusement park called Liberty Kingdom.

It had closed twelve years earlier after what Wikipedia described as “a series of unrelated electrical, animal, and promotional incidents.”

Adam nodded.

“That sounds good.”

Susan continued.

“The wildlife center received this anonymously this morning.”

She played an audio recording.

A distorted voice said:

“IF YOU WANT YOUR PRECIOUS SYMBOL OF FREEDOM BACK, COME TO LIBERTY KINGDOM BEFORE MIDNIGHT.”

Pause.

“BRING SIX HUNDRED HOT DOGS.”

Silence.

Dave looked around.

“Now see, the eagle kidnapping I was following. The hot dogs make me feel like we are entering somebody else’s problem.”

Ace narrowed his eyes.

“Animals.”

Susan nodded.

“We think whoever took Larry may have other animals there.”

Ace slowly smiled.

Then made a dolphin noise.

Susan looked at Michael.

“Why is he here?”

Michael whispered:

“He’s the animal guy.”

“I know who he is.”

“Oh.”

Jim Carrey, who had been completely silent so far, suddenly leaned forward.

“What kind of hot dogs?”

Everyone turned.

Susan blinked.

“What?”

“Beef? Pork? Chicken? Mystery?”

“I don’t know.”

Carrey slowly reclined.

“Then we know less than we thought.”

Dave pointed at him.

“See, that right there is why you don’t invite certain people to briefings.”

—

They arrived at Liberty Kingdom at 8:42 p.m.

The abandoned amusement park rose behind rusted gates like Disneyland after losing a custody battle.

A faded sign read:

LIBERTY KINGDOM: AMERICA’S MOST PATRIOTIC FAMILY EXPERIENCE

Underneath, graffiti had added:

NOT ANYMORE

Will Ferrell stared upward.

“This place still has dignity.”

Danny DeVito looked at the collapsed roller coaster.

“No it doesn’t.”

Michael gathered everyone around.

“Okay. Team assignments.”

Dave immediately said:

“No.”

Michael ignored him.

“Ace, animals.”

Ace saluted.

“Sandler, morale.”

Adam nodded.

“I can do sandwiches too.”

“Jim, infiltration.”

Carrey instantly transformed his posture and began walking like an elderly maintenance worker.

Michael pointed.

“Exactly.”

Dave asked:

“What am I doing?”

Michael looked down at his notes.

“Cultural analysis.”

Dave stared.

“What does that mean?”

“I don’t know, but it sounded respectful.”

Dave nodded.

“That was smart.”

“Thank you.”

“Still not doing it.”

Ferrell raised his hand.

“What is my assignment?”

Michael looked at him.

“Security.”

Ferrell inhaled deeply.

Finally.

His purpose.

“Understood.”

He immediately picked up a traffic cone and placed it on his head.

Susan said:

“What are you doing?”

Ferrell whispered:

“Becoming difficult to classify.”

Dave closed his eyes.

—

They entered through a broken gift shop.

Old patriotic merchandise lay everywhere.

Plastic Liberty Bells.

Foam Uncle Sam hats.

Stuffed eagles.

A vending machine still displayed a faded Mountain Dew logo.

Danny DeVito stopped.

“Hold on.”

He approached the vending machine.

Everyone kept walking.

Five seconds later:

“SON OF A—”

They turned.

His dollar had been eaten.

DeVito slapped the machine.

“You think because society collapsed there are no laws?”

Dave pointed at him.

“Danny, there is literally nobody working here.”

“That makes it worse.”

Adam walked over.

“You want me to shake it?”

“No.”

Carrey appeared beside them.

“I’ll negotiate.”

He leaned toward the vending machine.

“Sir.”

Silence.

“You’ve taken something that does not belong to you.”

Silence.

“We can do this easy.”

Silence.

“Or we can do this… carbonated.”

He kicked the machine.

A Sprite fell out.

DeVito picked it up.

“That’s not what I ordered.”

Dave walked away.

—

Ace found the first clue near the old carousel.

Droppings.

He crouched.

Touched one.

Smelled it.

Susan recoiled.

“Please don’t—”

Ace licked his finger.

Susan gagged.

Ace frowned.

“Corn.”

Dave said:

“You could’ve just said corn.”

“Where’s the craftsmanship in that?”

Ace stood.

“Goats.”

Susan blinked.

“Goats?”

“Six, maybe seven.”

“How do you know?”

Ace pointed.

A goat was standing twenty feet away.

Everyone looked at it.

Ace lowered his hand.

“That also.”

The goat was wearing a small American flag cape.

Will Ferrell whispered:

“My God.”

The goat stared at him.

Ferrell saluted.

The goat ate part of a map.

Ferrell lowered his hand.

“Traitor.”

—

They followed the goat into an old Wild West attraction.

Behind the fake saloon were dozens of animals.

Goats.

Two miniature horses.

Three llamas.

A pig.

Several chickens.

And an enormous tortoise wearing a sheriff badge.

Ace was overwhelmed.

“Look at them.”

Dave looked around.

“This is either animal trafficking or the weirdest county fair ever.”

A speaker crackled overhead.

The distorted voice returned.

“WELCOME, INTRUDERS.”

Everyone froze.

“YOU HAVE ENTERED THE DOMAIN OF—”

The audio glitched.

“—THE DOMAIN OF—”

More static.

Then a normal voice:

“Hold on. Gary, is this thing on?”

A second voice:

“Yeah.”

“No, the distortion stopped.”

“You leaned on something.”

“Which button?”

“Blue one.”

“THIS ONE?”

The voice became demonic again.

“YES.”

Dave stared upward.

“This villain has technical support.”

The distorted voice continued.

“YOU WILL NEVER FIND PRESIDENT LARRY.”

Ace shouted:

“WHERE IS THE BIRD?”

“YOU WILL DISCOVER THAT SOON ENOUGH.”

“IS HE SAFE?”

Pause.

“Yes.”

Ace relaxed.

“Okay.”

Everyone stared.

Ace shrugged.

“What? That’s my main concern.”

—

The group split up.

This lasted three minutes.

Michael immediately reorganized everyone into a “synergy pod.”

Dave left the pod.

Ferrell declared himself “forward reconnaissance” and became lost inside a mirror maze.

Adam and DeVito found the abandoned concession stand and began making hot dogs.

Susan tried calling law enforcement.

There was no signal.

Jim Carrey somehow found an old security uniform and had already convinced himself he worked there.

He approached Michael.

“Sir, I’m going to need to see your park authorization.”

Michael looked delighted.

“Oh, we’re doing a bit.”

Carrey remained perfectly serious.

“Authorization.”

Michael laughed.

Carrey did not.

Michael slowly stopped laughing.

“I don’t have one.”

“Then I’m going to have to escort you off the premises.”

“But we came together.”

“Sir.”

“Jim.”

“Sir.”

“Jim.”

Carrey leaned closer.

“My name is Carl.”

Michael looked genuinely uncertain.

Dave watched from a bench.

“This is why civilization has rules.”

—

Meanwhile, Ferrell had been trapped in the mirror maze for fifteen minutes.

He was becoming increasingly convinced that the reflections represented rival operatives.

“Stand down.”

Reflection Ferrell copied him.

Ferrell narrowed his eyes.

“Oh, you’re good.”

He sidestepped.

Reflection matched.

“Military training.”

He lunged.

He hit the glass.

“SON OF A—”

From somewhere behind him came a voice.

“Will?”

Ferrell spun.

Nicolas Cage stepped into view.

No explanation.

Just Nicolas Cage.

Ferrell stared.

“Nicolas.”

Cage nodded.

“Will.”

“What are you doing here?”

Cage looked around at the mirrors.

“I followed a coupon.”

Ferrell accepted this immediately.

“There’s an eagle.”

“I know.”

“How?”

Cage stared at his own reflection.

“Because the park wants us to think this is about the eagle.”

Ferrell gasped.

“What is it actually about?”

Cage turned.

“Hot dogs.”

Ferrell’s mouth fell open.

Of course.

—

Back at the concession stand, Adam Sandler had prepared 412 hot dogs.

DeVito was counting them.

“Four twelve.”

“We need six hundred.”

“Yeah.”

“We’re out of buns.”

“So?”

DeVito stared at him.

“You can’t hand a criminal naked hot dogs.”

Adam shrugged.

“I’ve been to worse parties.”

Dave entered.

“Why are you actually making the hot dogs?”

Adam looked at him.

“The guy asked for them.”

Dave stared.

“You know, in every hostage movie there’s one character that misunderstands the assignment so completely it becomes useful.”

“Thanks.”

“That wasn’t a compliment.”

“I know.”

Then they heard music.

Everyone stopped.

Old speakers throughout the park crackled to life.

A marching band version of “Yankee Doodle” began playing.

Lights flickered on.

The Ferris wheel started moving.

One by one, attractions lit up across the park.

Dave slowly looked around.

“Oh, hell no.”

Michael emerged wearing a foam Uncle Sam hat.

“What?”

“This is Act Three lighting.”

Michael nodded as if that made sense.

—

They converged at the central plaza.

A massive stage stood beneath a fiberglass bald eagle.

On the stage was President Larry.

Safe.

Perched comfortably.

Beside him stood two men.

One wore the groundhog costume.

The other wore a headset and held a clipboard.

The headset man stepped forward.

“Welcome.”

Susan pointed.

“You stole our eagle!”

“Borrowed.”

“YOU CAN’T BORROW AN EAGLE.”

“Technically—”

Ace screamed:

“DO NOT SAY TECHNICALLY.”

The man flinched.

“Okay.”

He removed his headset.

“My name is Gary. I was assistant events director here before Liberty Kingdom closed.”

Dave nodded.

“There it is.”

Gary gestured toward the animals.

“When the park shut down, some of the animal exhibits were transferred. Some weren’t.”

Susan frowned.

“So you’ve been taking care of them?”

“For twelve years.”

Everyone looked around.

The goats.

Llamas.

Pig.

Tortoise.

President Larry.

Ace’s anger softened slightly.

Gary continued.

“The property was sold last month. Developers are coming tomorrow. Nobody cared about the animals.”

“So you kidnapped a federally protected eagle?” Susan asked.

Gary looked ashamed.

“I needed attention.”

Dave slowly nodded.

“See, this is how bad marketing happens.”

Michael stepped forward.

“You needed awareness.”

Gary nodded.

“You needed engagement.”

“Yes.”

“You needed stakeholder buy-in.”

“Yes!”

Michael’s eyes lit up.

“I understand you.”

Dave immediately said:

“No. Nobody let those two collaborate.”

Too late.

Michael hugged Gary.

—

Then Cage and Ferrell arrived.

Cage stepped into the plaza.

“I know what this is really about.”

Everyone turned.

Gary looked confused.

“What?”

Cage pointed dramatically toward the 412 hot dogs.

“The hot dogs.”

Gary stared.

“No. Those were for the animals and volunteers.”

Cage paused.

“Oh.”

Ferrell looked devastated.

“That’s it?”

“Yes.”

“No hidden code?”

“No.”

“Six hundred isn’t coordinates?”

“No.”

“Hot dog length times six hundred doesn’t reveal an underground vault?”

Gary frowned.

“What?”

Cage slowly lowered his hand.

“Nothing.”

Dave walked up to him.

“You Nicolas Cage?”

“Yes.”

“What are you doing here?”

“I followed a coupon.”

Dave stared for a second.

“Fair enough.”

—

Then President Larry flew away.

Everyone watched.

The eagle rose from the stage.

Circled once.

And landed on top of the Ferris wheel.

Ace whispered:

“Oh, you magnificent bastard.”

Susan panicked.

“We need him back.”

Michael pointed dramatically.

“Team!”

Dave said:

“No.”

Michael continued.

“This is what we trained for.”

“We have never trained.”

“Exactly. They won’t expect us.”

“Who is they?”

Michael pointed at the sky.

“The elements.”

Dave walked away again.

Ace began calculating routes.

Susan retrieved handling equipment.

Carrey somehow had a cherry picker.

Nobody knew where he got it.

Ferrell insisted he could climb the Ferris wheel “because upper body strength is mostly belief.”

He made it six feet.

Adam began throwing bits of hot dog onto the ground.

Susan shouted:

“Do not feed the eagle hot dogs!”

Adam stopped.

“Okay.”

DeVito took the hot dog.

“No reason to waste it.”

Cage watched the eagle carefully.

Then he noticed the tortoise.

Sheriff tortoise.

Slowly moving across the plaza.

Cage narrowed his eyes.

“What does he know?”

Dave said:

“He’s a tortoise.”

“Exactly.”

“What does that mean?”

“I don’t know yet.”

Dave rubbed his forehead.

—

Finally Ace stepped forward.

Everyone quieted.

He made a soft bird call.

President Larry looked down.

Ace called again.

Larry shifted.

Again.

The eagle spread its wings.

Everyone held their breath.

Larry flew from the Ferris wheel.

Glided downward.

And landed—

directly on Michael Scott’s head.

Michael froze.

The talons gripped his foam Uncle Sam hat.

Everyone stared.

Michael whispered:

“Nobody move.”

Dave started laughing.

Michael’s eyes filled with tears.

“He chose me.”

Susan said:

“He chose the hat.”

“He chose what the hat represents.”

“He thinks it’s a perch.”

Michael looked deeply offended.

Ace carefully recovered Larry.

The eagle was safe.

The animals were transferred to legitimate sanctuaries.

Gary was arrested, although Susan admitted there would probably be “a complicated conversation with the district attorney.”

Liberty Kingdom was shut down for good.

Almost.

Because two weeks later, Michael Scott purchased a minority stake in the property.

Dave found out through a text message.

He immediately called him.

“Why?”

Michael answered cheerfully.

“Team-building retreat.”

“No.”

“Wildlife sanctuary slash leadership campus.”

“No.”

“Dave, imagine corporate America learning communication from llamas.”

“No.”

“We already booked the tortoise.”

Dave hung up.

—

Six months later, Liberty Kingdom reopened under a new name:

FREEDOM SYNERGY WILDLIFE & LEADERSHIP EXPERIENCE

Attendance was terrible.

Reviews were mixed.

The tortoise became extremely popular.

Ferrell was banned after challenging a llama to “psychological combat.”

Carrey still occasionally showed up dressed as Carl from security.

DeVito sued the vending machine company and won eleven dollars.

Ace visited President Larry every month.

Sandler opened a concession stand that only sold hot dogs and grilled cheese.

And Nicolas Cage purchased one annual pass.

Nobody knew why.

One afternoon, Dave found Cage standing alone beneath the old Ferris wheel, staring at President Larry circling overhead.

Dave walked up.

“You figuring something out?”

Cage nodded.

“What?”

Cage watched the eagle.

“For six months I’ve been trying to determine why the ransom demand specified six hundred hot dogs.”

Dave stared at him.

“Gary told you.”

“Yes.”

“They were for the volunteers.”

“I know.”

“So what are you trying to figure out?”

Cage slowly turned.

“Why six hundred?”

Dave stared.

Cage stared back.

From across the park, Michael yelled:

“FREE HOT DOGS AT SIX!”

Cage’s eyes widened.

Dave pointed at him.

“No.”

Cage whispered:

“Six.”

“No.”

“Six hundred.”

“Nicolas.”

“Six o’clock.”

“Nicolas.”

Cage began walking faster.

Dave grabbed his shoulder.

“Leave it alone.”

Cage looked at him.

For several seconds, there was genuine struggle in his eyes.

Then Cage exhaled.

“You’re right.”

Dave released him.

Cage walked away.

Ten feet later he stopped.

Turned.

“But what if—”

“GO HOME, NICOLAS.”

Cage nodded.

“Fair.”

President Larry screamed overhead.

Cage looked up.

Then back at Dave.

Dave pointed toward the exit.

Cage left.

Mostly.

That gives us several different comedy frequencies at once: Chappelle-style observational sanity, Michael Scott social incompetence, Ferrell’s heroic confidence applied to idiocy, Ace’s obsessive competence, Carrey’s commitment to a bit, DeVito’s petty pragmatism, Sandler’s casual man-child energy, and Cage as the pattern-addicted wild card.